            <ArticleTitle>Acute percutaneous transluminal coronary angioplasty complicated by embolism to a coronary artery remote from the site of infarction.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>266-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Percutaneous transluminal coronary angioplasty has emerged as a mode of treatment in patients with both chronic and acute coronary syndromes. Among the recognized complications of coronary angioplasty is coronary embolism. This case report describes a case of acute anterior myocardial infarction which was treated with angioplasty of the left anterior descending coronary artery. Postdilation angiography revealed obstruction of the posterior descending artery (in a left dominant system). The use of thrombolytic therapy previous to angioplasty is briefly discussed.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
